<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shō Sei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1497">1497</a>–<a href="/years/1555">1555</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sho Sei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shō Sei was king of Ryukyu from 1527 to 1555, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-shin">Shō Shin</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sho-dynasty">Second Shō dynasty</a>. He campaigned in the Amami Islands in 1537 to enforce royal authority in the north, and in his reign forts were built at the mouth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naha">Naha</a> harbour against wakō <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>. The kingdom remained prosperous, although its trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> was declining.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sho-sei.jpg" alt="Shō Sei" width="320" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sho-Sei-color.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
